  Task ID  : TASK-21CFR11-DD036611
  Domain   : 21cfr11
  Context  :
    At NovaBio Inc., a biopharma company, the IT department recently migrated
    historical data from the outdated LegacyLIMS v2.3 system to a new Laboratory
    Information Management System (LIMS). The migration involved reformating
    date fields from 'DD-MMM-YYYY' to 'YYYY-MM-DD', normalizing units of
    measurement for consistency, and mapping old column names to the new schema.
    A single bulk audit entry was recorded for each migration task: 'Batch
    migration from LegacyLIMS v2.3 on 2022-02-15'. Although the post-migration
    reconciliation scripts verified that record counts, checksums, and a 5%
    random sample spot-check matched perfectly, the QA lead certified the
    migration as 'validated and fully traceable'. Notably, the LegacyLIMS v2.3
    system was placed in read-only mode after the migration to preserve
    historical data integrity, which might raise initial concerns about data
    availability. However, this read-only setup is part of the company's
    compliant data retention policy.
  Question :
    Does the described data migration process at NovaBio Inc. comply with the
    FDA's requirements for electronic records and audit trails as specified in
    21 CFR Part 11, specifically regarding the granularity of audit trail
    entries for changes made during data migration?
  Gold Std :
    The process does not comply with 21 CFR 11.10(e), which requires that audit
    trails capture the original value, the new value, who made the change, when,
    and why, at the individual record/field level. The bulk migration log entry
    does not meet this requirement because it does not allow for the
    reconstruction of what any individual field contained before migration. The
    read-only mode of the LegacyLIMS v2.3 system after migration is a compliant
    data preservation measure and not the violation. The verification of record
    counts, checksums, and the random sample spot-check, while indicative of
